# Chroma content pack Science, modern Earth: THRESHOLD SEASONS for the main rungs (moments 52 to 67 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md,
# Part 5). Four short seasons of four linked moments each, for the first months after a person takes research scientist,
# research group leader, independent investigator or research facility lead. The crossing life events of
# science-events.lib ('the contract runs out', 'they want you to lead a group', 'a fellowship of your own', 'the facility
# needs a head') are the rites that open them; any other way into the title opens the season too.
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py, plus the season fields the engine accepted (chroma-packs/for-the-engine.md,
# "Engine answer"; chroma-library/drafts/next2/for-the-engine.md, section 3): threshold: title:<name> (a season that opens
# when that title is gained), step: 1 | 2 | 3 (in order over about 26 weeks: the crossing, the in-between, settling in),
# transform: yes (the season's transforming chance, whose acts move a larger share of the colors into the deep core).
# Every moment: tier inner, alpha even, stakes .8, holds: the title, the five one-color options and one full cross cycle;
# each season uses the cycles s1 to s4 once. Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md. Pathways and packs thread, 2026-10-05.

# ================================================================ research scientist (ages 24-80)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 52. step 1 (cycle s1)
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P2, P16): W>U signs the fixed-term postdoc ([postdoctoral researcher], the facet on the new
# scientist's post) for whoever has the doctorate (requires:, without: impossible), as most new doctors do.

== the first week with your own bench
tier: inner
threshold: title:research scientist
step: 1
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 24-80
times: once, in the first weeks after becoming a research scientist: nearly everyone who takes such a post meets it; about 1 life in 80 ever holds a research scientist post (estimate)
holds: research scientist
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, mentor
earth: the first week in the new post: a bench, a desk and a login of your own, and nobody yet saying what to do first
requires: in the threshold season of research scientist, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'the contract runs out', a finished doctorate or any other way in): step 1, the first weeks in the post
likelier: a new institute or a new city; a post won after a long search; the first post after a doctorate
rarer: a step up inside the same lab, at the same bench; a crisis at home that crowds out the new start
scene: On Monday {N} is shown a bench, half a shelf in the cold room and a desk by the window. By Wednesday nobody has said what to do first.
scene W: {N} reads the safety induction, the lab rules and the booking system from end to end, and signs every form before touching anything.
scene U: The empty bench is a blank page. {N} lists every question worth asking here and sorts them by how long each would take to answer.
scene B: Whoever claims the good shelf, the freezer drawer and a slot on the shared machine in the first week keeps them for years. {N} has noticed.
scene R: {N} unpacks a box of old notebooks onto the bench and wants to start something, anything, this afternoon.
scene G: The bench still carries the last owner's labels, and the plant on the windowsill has not been watered in weeks. {N} waters it, and feels for the rhythm of the place before changing anything.
- W | do every induction, rule and booking form before starting any work | v: conformity, security | chance: 92
- U | spend the week reading the lab's old notebooks and the papers behind them | door | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 85
- B | claim the freezer drawer, the good shelf and a weekly slot on the shared machine | v: power, security | chance: 85
- R | start a quick pilot experiment on Wednesday, before anyone sets a plan | v: stimulation | chance: 79
- G | ask the oldest technician, over tea, how the lab really runs | v: tradition | mark: made a friend | chance: 80
- W>U | sign the fixed-term postdoc contract, and agree a three-year plan with the boss | title: postdoctoral researcher | requires: doctoral graduate | without: impossible | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 84
- U>B | map who controls the money, the machines and the hiring before choosing a project | v: power, security | chance: 82
- B>R | book the big machine for the quiet Friday nights nobody else wants | habit | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 85
- R>G | put up photographs, bring in a kettle and make the bench feel like home | v: hedonism, security | chance: 88
- G>W | keep the last owner's labels and routines until you understand why they are there | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 90
works: By Friday the bench has a plan on it, and {N} knows whom to ask for what.
works: {boss} stops by at the end of the week, looks around and says it is a good start.
fails: The week goes on forms, broken logins and waiting, and nothing gets started.
fails: Something done in a hurry rubs someone the wrong way, and the corridor remembers.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 53. step 2 (cycle s2)

== a question of your own, or the boss's
tier: inner
threshold: title:research scientist
step: 2
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 24-80
times: once, in the first months after becoming a research scientist: most first posts are paid from a supervisor's grant, and perhaps half of new scientists come to care about another question that soon; across all lives about 1 in 160 (estimate)
holds: research scientist
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, mentor, colleague
earth: your post is paid from the boss's grant, which asks one question, and you have started to care about another
requires: in the threshold season of research scientist, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, some weeks into the post, while the salary comes from someone else's grant
likelier: a post on a supervisor's grant, as most first posts are; an idea carried over from the doctorate; a supervisor who leaves little room
rarer: a fellowship or a permanent post of one's own; a supervisor who already shares the person's question
scene: The grant that pays {Ns} salary asks one question. Over the past weeks {N} has fallen for a different one, and {boss} wants a work plan by the end of the month.
scene W: The money was given for a stated piece of work, and the funders were promised it. {N} feels the weight of that promise, even with another idea burning.
scene U: {Ns} own question is cleaner and might explain more, but it would take a year to know. {N} sketches both designs side by side on one page.
scene B: Papers on the boss's question will carry the boss's name. A question of {Ns} own is what a career is built on.
scene R: The new idea keeps {N} awake at night. The funded question has started to feel like homework.
scene G: The lab has worked on the boss's question for twelve years, with samples and records going back to the start. {Ns} idea would grow better from those roots than from nothing.
- W | do the funded work as promised, and keep your idea for the next grant round | v: conformity, security | mark: kept your word | self_control: + | chance: 88
- U | design one experiment that could answer both questions at once | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 45
- B | tell the boss you will stay only if one day a week is your own | v: power, self-direction | chance: 48
- R | work on your own idea every evening and weekend, for the love of it | habit | v: stimulation, self-direction | self_control: + | chance: 82
- G | look back through the lab's twelve years of records for where your question already shows | v: tradition, achievement | chance: 48
- W>B | put your idea into the grant's next report, with your name on it | v: achievement, power | chance: 60
- U>R | trim the outliers from your evening pilot until your idea looks too good to drop | v: self-direction | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: selective analysis of research data; backfire: the boss asks for a clean rerun and the effect is gone | self_control: - | chance: 90
- B>G | do the boss's work, and save every leftover sample toward your own question later | v: security, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 90
- R>W | say plainly at the lab meeting that the funded question is the wrong one | v: universalism, self-direction | mark: defied an authority | chance: 45
- G>U | ask your old mentor which of the two questions will still matter in twenty years | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 84
works: {N} and {boss} agree on a split that keeps the funded work going and lets the new question start.
works: By the end of the month {N} has a plan that feels like {Ns} own.
fails: {boss} says no, and the new idea goes back in a drawer.
fails: The month goes on arguing and second-guessing, and neither question moves.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 54. step 2, transform (cycle s3)

== the experiment that will define you
tier: inner
threshold: title:research scientist
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 24-80
times: once, in the first months after becoming a research scientist, for the perhaps 1 in 3 given money and time to choose one big piece of work; across all lives about 1 in 250 (estimate)
holds: research scientist
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: years
roles: boss, mentor, rival, colleague
earth: there is money and time for one big experiment this year, and how you choose it will set what kind of scientist you become
requires: in the threshold season of research scientist, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, the season's transforming chance, once there is some money and time to choose the big piece of work
likelier: a fellowship, a start-up fund or a boss who gives real freedom; two good ideas competing; a rival on the same problem
rarer: a post with no say over the work; money too tight for anything but the funded plan
scene: There is money and time for one big piece of work this year. Whatever {N} chooses will fill the next three years and become what people mean when they say {Ns} name.
scene W: {N} thinks of the doctors, farmers and planners who may one day rely on this result. It has to be something they can trust.
scene U: Two explanations fit everything known so far. {N} has spent a week designing the one experiment that could tell them apart.
scene B: {rival} is working on the same problem two floors up. Whoever owns the method first will own the field for a decade.
scene R: There is a wild idea {N} cannot shake, the kind supervisors talk students out of. Now there is no supervisor to do the talking.
scene G: {N} keeps coming back to one {place}, one population, one slow process. The experiment that matters there would take years of patience.
- W | design a large preregistered study that others can repeat and rely on | identity | binds | v: universalism, conformity | grants: research integrity | chance: 65
- U | build the cleanest test between the two explanations, whatever it shows | identity | v: self-direction, universalism | grants: experimental design | chance: 68
- B | develop a method nobody else has, and keep the details close until it is out | identity | v: power, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 62
- R | bet the year on the odd idea that excites you, against all advice | identity | binds | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 50
- G | commit to ten years of watching one place through every season | identity | binds | v: tradition, universalism | grants: fieldwork | chance: 70
- W>R | take on the urgent problem that moves you most, and do it strictly by the book | identity | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 48
- U>G | pair models with years of close watching of one living system | identity | binds | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 68
- B>W | sign on as the junior partner in a big consortium, and make yourself its indispensable expert | identity | binds | v: power, achievement | chance: 70
- R>U | teach yourself a whole new method in a month, because the question demands it | identity | door | v: stimulation, achievement | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 72
- G>B | build your speciality around the lab's irreplaceable old collection, and guard it | identity | v: power | chance: 65
works: The first results come in on schedule, and they are solid enough to build three years on.
works: Within the year people in the field start to say what {N} stands for, and they say it right.
fails: The experiment eats the year and gives back nothing that can be used.
fails: Halfway through, {N} sees the plan was wrong for the question, and has to start again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 55. step 3 (cycle s4)

== half a year in, the work has a shape
tier: inner
threshold: title:research scientist
step: 3
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 24-80
times: once, about six months after becoming a research scientist, for most who are still in the post; about 1 life in 80 ever holds a research scientist post (estimate)
holds: research scientist
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, friend
earth: half a year into the post, the weeks have found a pattern: what you do, when, and with whom
requires: in the threshold season of research scientist, within the first six months after taking the title: step 3, near the end of the six months, as the new life settles
likelier: a steady post and a first small result; colleagues the person gets on with
rarer: a crisis in the lab or at home; a post already ending
scene: Six months in, {N} notices that the weeks have a shape: Mondays at the bench, Thursdays on the analysis, a lab meeting that no longer sets {Ns} heart racing.
scene W: {Ns} lab notebook is up to date to the day, and {colleague} has started borrowing it as a model.
scene U: A first small result is in. It is not exciting, but {N} understands exactly why it came out the way it did.
scene B: {N} has worked out who in the building gets things done and who only talks. The list is short and useful.
scene R: Some days the work still feels like play. Other days {N} misses the fizz of the first week.
scene G: The plant on the windowsill has made it through the summer. {N} has stopped feeling like the new one.
- W | set a fixed weekly routine and keep to it, even in the busy weeks | habit | v: conformity, security | self_control: + | chance: 82
- U | write up the small result properly, though nobody has asked for it yet | v: achievement | self_control: + | chance: 92
- B | drop the duties that do nothing for your work, politely and for good | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 80
- R | take the long weekend you promised yourself, and go somewhere wild | door | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 92
- G | let the work settle into its own pace instead of pushing it | v: tradition | chance: 75
- W>G | start a monthly lunch for the corridor, so new people are not left alone | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 80
- U>W | turn your notes into a protocol the whole lab can follow | v: universalism, achievement | grants: reproducible workflow | chance: 66
- B>U | trade your spare machine hours for lessons from the statistics expert | door | v: achievement, self-direction | grants: statistical judgment | chance: 75
- R>B | volunteer for the conference talk nobody else wants to give | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 85
- G>R | spend Friday afternoons in the greenhouse or the field, just watching, for joy | habit | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 88
works: The weeks keep their shape, and the work starts to move without being pushed.
works: At the lab meeting {boss} mentions how far {N} has come since the first week.
fails: A deadline from nowhere knocks the new routine flat within a fortnight.
fails: The pattern turns out to be a rut, and {N} is restless by the end of the month.

# ================================================================ research group leader (ages 30-80)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 56. step 1 (cycle s1)

== keys to an empty lab
tier: inner
threshold: title:research group leader
step: 1
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 30-80
times: once, in the first weeks after becoming a research group leader, for the perhaps 1 in 2 who fit out new rooms; about 1 life in 500 ever leads a research group, so about 1 in 1,000 meet it (estimate)
holds: research group leader
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, elder
earth: you are handed the keys to an empty lab, a start-up budget and a catalogue of equipment to order
requires: in the threshold season of research group leader, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'they want you to lead a group' or any other appointment): step 1, the first weeks, with new rooms to fit out
likelier: a new building or a move to a new institute; a start-up budget that comes with the post
rarer: a group that keeps its old rooms and kit; a post with no money of its own
scene: The lab is bare: benches, sockets, a sink and an echo. {N} holds the keys, a start-up budget and a catalogue of equipment, and nobody else is here yet.
scene W: Before the first order goes in, {N} reads the institute's rules on purchasing, safety and hiring, and opens a file for each.
scene U: {N} paces the room with a tape measure, working out where every instrument should stand so the work flows from door to window.
scene B: The start-up money is the most {N} will ever spend without asking anyone. Every choice now is leverage later.
scene R: {N} stands in the middle of the empty room and laughs out loud. It is all {Ns} to fill.
scene G: The room still smells faintly of the group that worked here for thirty years. A faded photograph of them is pinned inside a cupboard door.
- W | open a safety file and a purchase log before buying a single pipette | v: conformity, security | chance: 92
- U | plan the layout around how the work will flow, then order to the plan | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 92
- B | spend most of the start-up money early, before the department can claw it back | v: power, security | chance: 90
- R | order the one big instrument you have dreamed of, and worry about the rest later | v: stimulation | chance: 80
- G | ask a retired member of the old group what the room was good for | v: tradition | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- W>U | ask three established group leaders what they wish they had bought first | door | v: conformity, achievement | chance: 82
- U>B | compare every quote and play the suppliers off against each other for discounts | v: power, achievement | chance: 82
- B>R | borrow a colleague's spare kit for a year, and spend the savings on something bold | v: stimulation, power | chance: 72
- R>G | paint the walls and bring in plants, so the people who come will want to stay | body: light | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 85
- G>W | buy only what the first year needs, and keep the rest for the group to come | v: security, benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 80
works: By the end of the week the orders are in and the room has a plan pinned to the wall.
works: The first boxes arrive, and the empty lab starts to look like a place where work will happen.
fails: An order goes wrong, and half the budget is tied up in a machine that will not fit through the door.
fails: The department freezes purchases for the quarter, and the room stays empty.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 57. step 2 (cycle s2)

== hiring the first two people
tier: inner
threshold: title:research group leader
step: 2
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 30-80
times: once, in the first months after becoming a research group leader; most new leaders fill their first posts within the first year; about 1 life in 500 ever leads a research group (estimate)
holds: research group leader
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, rival, mentor, friend
earth: two posts are open, the first you have ever filled, and the people you pick will shape the group for years
requires: in the threshold season of research group leader, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, some weeks in, with posts to fill
likelier: a start-up budget with posts in it; people who left with the old leader; a field with many applicants
rarer: a group already full; a hiring freeze at the institute
scene: Two posts, forty applications. The first people {N} chooses will set the tone of the group for years, and {N} has never hired anyone.
scene W: {N} writes the criteria before reading a single application, so every candidate is judged the same way.
scene U: One candidate has a flawless record. Another has a strange, brilliant thesis and a two-year gap. {N} reads both twice.
scene B: A former student of {rival} has applied. Hiring them would bring {rival}'s methods and contacts along.
scene R: In the interview one candidate lights up talking about a failed experiment, and {N} feels the room change.
scene G: {N} thinks less about the best candidate on paper than about who will still be here, and still be kind, in five years.
- W | score every candidate against the written criteria, and hire the top two | v: conformity, universalism | chance: 72
- U | set every finalist a real problem from the lab, and judge how they think | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 75
- B | hire the rival's former student, with the methods and contacts they bring | v: power, achievement | chance: 68
- R | hire the one who lit up in the interview, on instinct | v: stimulation | chance: 58
- G | hire someone from the lab you trained in, whose people you know | v: tradition | chance: 75
- W>B | use the institute's fast-track rules to sign the best candidate before other labs bid | v: power, achievement | chance: 65
- U>R | take a chance on the odd thesis with the gap, after checking it properly | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 48
- B>G | quietly give one post to a friend's partner who needs work in town | v: security, benevolence | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a post filled around the fair process; backfire: a passed-over candidate complains and the hire is reviewed | self_control: - | chance: 88
- R>W | ring every candidate you turn down, and tell each one honestly why | v: benevolence, universalism | self_control: + | chance: 80
- G>U | ask the finalists' old supervisors, quietly, how each one copes in a bad month | v: tradition, security | chance: 72
works: The two people who start in the new year turn out to be the ones the group needed.
works: Word gets round that {N} hires fairly, and the next round of applicants is stronger.
fails: One of the new hires leaves within three months, and the post has to be filled again.
fails: The choice causes a quarrel in the department, and {N} starts the group under a cloud.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 58. step 2, transform (cycle s3)

== the culture you set now
tier: inner
threshold: title:research group leader
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 30-80
times: once, in the first months after becoming a research group leader; nearly every new leader sets the group's habits then, knowingly or not; about 1 life in 500 ever leads a research group (estimate)
holds: research group leader
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: years
roles: colleague, mentor, boss
earth: the group is new under you, and the habits you set in these months will last as long as the group does
requires: in the threshold season of research group leader, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, the season's transforming chance, once the first people are in place
likelier: a new group or a group that lost its old leader; the person's memory of labs that worked and labs that did not
rarer: a group so large that its ways were set long ago; a leader who is rarely there
scene: The group is new to {N}, and {N} is new to it. How meetings run, who gets the credit, what happens after a mistake: whatever {N} does in these months will still be the group's way in ten years.
scene W: {N} drafts a page of group rules: authorship, data, hours, how disagreements are settled. It feels stiff, and necessary.
scene U: {N} wants a group where every claim is challenged, every method explained and every result checked twice, and wonders how to build that without fear.
scene B: Funders back groups that deliver. {N} thinks about targets, deadlines and whose name goes where.
scene R: {N} remembers the labs that crushed people, and the one lab that felt like a band on tour. Only one kind is worth building.
scene G: {N} thinks of the group as something to grow, like a family or an orchard: slow, rooted, looked after.
- W | write clear rules on credit, data and hours, and apply them to yourself first | identity | binds | v: conformity, universalism | grants: credit negotiation | chance: 85
- U | make every result face a hard internal review before it leaves the room | identity | v: achievement, universalism | grants: research integrity | chance: 72
- B | set high targets, reward whoever delivers, and let the rest find other labs | identity | v: power, achievement | chance: 80
- R | run the group loose and fiery: open doors, wild ideas, arguments over a beer | identity | habit | v: stimulation, hedonism | grants: a loyal research team | chance: 69
- G | treat the group like a family: birthdays, slow growth, nobody left behind | identity | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | grants: supervising researchers | chance: 70
- W>R | commit the group to a hard public problem, and let that duty fire everyone up | identity | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 48
- U>G | build slow, careful studies the group can grow with for decades | identity | binds | v: universalism, tradition | chance: 66
- B>W | trade favours with the department heads until the group has a protected place | identity | binds | v: power, security | chance: 72
- R>U | promise every member a year of freedom on a question of their own | identity | binds | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 72
- G>B | keep the group small and self-reliant, beholden to no big consortium | identity | v: self-direction, security | chance: 70
works: The habits take, and new people pick them up without being told.
works: A year on, people outside the group can say what it stands for, and they are right.
fails: The rules feel like a cage, or the freedom like chaos, and the first member starts looking elsewhere.
fails: What {N} meant to build and what the group became drift apart, and nobody says so.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 59. step 3 (cycle s4)

== the group meeting runs itself
tier: inner
threshold: title:research group leader
step: 3
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 30-80
times: once, near six months after becoming a research group leader, for the perhaps 1 in 2 whose group settles that soon; across all lives about 1 in 1,000 (estimate)
holds: research group leader
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: months
roles: colleague
earth: one Tuesday the group meeting starts, runs and ends well without you steering it
requires: in the threshold season of research group leader, within the first six months after taking the title: step 3, near the end of the six months, as the group settles
likelier: a group with a few months of shared work behind it; people who get on
rarer: a quarrel in the group; a member leaving; a leader away most weeks
scene: One Tuesday {N} arrives late to the group meeting and finds it already under way: {colleague} in the chair, the agenda followed, a disagreement settled. Nobody needed {N} to steer.
scene W: The rota, the minutes, the order of speakers: everything {N} set up in the first weeks is simply how things are done now.
scene U: {N} listens from the back as two juniors argue a point of method better than {N} could have a year ago.
scene B: A group that runs without its leader is either a triumph or a sign that the leader is no longer needed. {N} weighs which.
scene R: {N} feels a sudden pang: the group is growing up, and the wild first months are over.
scene G: Like a hedge that has finally taken, the group holds its own shape now, and {N} only needs to tend it.
- W | hand the chair to a different member each month, by rota | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 82
- U | use the freed hours to get back to your own analysis | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 92
- B | step in and take the chair back, so nobody forgets who leads | v: power | chance: 92
- R | skip next week's meeting and spend the morning at the bench | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 86
- G | sit back and let the group find its own way, saying little | v: tradition, benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 72
- W>G | set up a welcome pack and a buddy for every new member | v: benevolence, security | chance: 80
- U>W | ask the group to review your own work as hard as each other's | v: universalism | chance: 78
- B>U | court a big collaborator for data the group could never get alone | door | v: achievement, power | chance: 45
- R>B | throw the group a party for its first result, and invite the dean | v: hedonism, power | chance: 75
- G>R | walk the long way home and let yourself be glad, for once | v: hedonism | chance: 90
works: The meetings keep running well, and {N} gets a morning a week back for the work.
works: {colleague} says, half joking, that this is the best-run group in the building.
fails: The calm lasts a fortnight, then an old quarrel flares and everyone looks to {N} again.
fails: {N} steps back a little too far, and a decision gets made that {N} would never have made.

# ================================================================ independent investigator (ages 25-95)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 60. step 1 (cycle s1)

== nobody to report to on Monday
tier: inner
threshold: title:independent investigator
step: 1
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-95
times: once, in the first weeks after becoming an independent investigator: nearly everyone who goes it alone meets it; about 1 life in 2,000 ever works as an independent investigator (estimate)
holds: independent investigator
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: week
roles: friend, partner, mentor
earth: the fellowship has started, and on Monday morning there is nobody to report to and nobody to say what to do
requires: in the threshold season of independent investigator, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'a fellowship of your own', savings, a patron or any other way in): step 1, the first weeks working alone
likelier: years spent on other people's projects before this; money in the account for several years
rarer: an investigator who has worked alone before; money that covers only months
scene: Monday morning. The fellowship has started, the money is in the account, and for the first time in {Ns} working life nobody expects {N} anywhere at nine.
scene W: {N} writes out the fellowship's terms on one page and pins it above the desk: what was promised, by when, and to whom.
scene U: Five years, one question and nobody to interrupt. {N} opens a fresh notebook and writes the question at the top of the first page.
scene B: The fellowship is money, time and a name. {N} thinks about how to turn five years of freedom into a position nobody can take away.
scene R: {N} sleeps until ten, makes a huge breakfast, and then works until two in the morning because the idea will not wait.
scene G: {N} sets the desk by a window that looks out toward the {place} the work is about, and watches the light change before starting.
